    "_comment_sensitiveNameGlobs": "The secret/credentials/token name rules below are anchored on separators rather than written as substring globs (*secret* and friends). A substring glob matches the keyword anywhere in the path, so it blocked ordinary source and prose files — secretsmanager.tf, SecretsProvider.java, aws-credentials-setup.md, tokenizer.py — and there is no way to carve an exception: a deny rule cannot carry an allowlist, and no other settings scope can override a managed deny. The anchored forms match the naming convention real credential files follow (secrets.yaml, prod-secrets.json, db_secret.txt, api.credentials) and leave the keyword-as-a-word cases alone. Enforcement reaches Bash as well as the file tools, because Read and Edit deny rules are merged into the sandbox filesystem boundary. Two deliberate gaps: a keyword with text after it and no separator (aws_credentials_backup.txt) is not matched, and 'token' carries only the suffix and extension forms, because token.*/tokens.* collide with frontend design-token files. Values in both cases stay covered by output-redact.sh.",
